{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head 1c75f9cb7aae7f709fd95600c658571f816371ac, primary artifact d9771341 at cmd/gitseq-mcp, with d03c1b07 at internal/nexus, 07f04fa8 at internal/service and 81e21c35 at docs. All four are live, not stale, and name this commit; I checked that before promising rather than after reviewing.\n\nMECHANICAL. The head resolves, is a strict descendant of main ff6132ec, the worktree is clean, git diff --check is clean, and the pushed head matches. go vet and go build pass.\n\nCARRY-ONLY, verified by execution. git diff cb2f8d47 HEAD over cmd/gitseq-mcp, internal/nexus, internal/service and docs returns nothing, so integrating onto the newly shipped citation-guard main introduced no semantic drift in the reviewed paths.\n\nTHE CONTRACT. The refusal is gone: sharedIdentityError and the fatal path at startup are removed, and attend now warns instead of failing. That matches Hugh's ruling and planner's caf6d5e8, and the stated rationale holds — durable records carry the actor fingerprint, and review independence is enforced by fingerprint, so two sessions of one identity cannot manufacture an independent review by spelling.\n\nThe full-fingerprint work is a real correction, not a rename. The old check parsed a human display label and compared a twelve-character fingerprint prefix; the new one asks the resident for a count keyed on the exact fingerprint. TestLiveSessionCountUsesTheFullFingerprint pins exactly that: two actors sharing a twelve-character prefix are counted apart, and a bare prefix matches nothing.\n\nAggregate-only privacy holds structurally, not just by intent. Hub.LiveSessionsForActor returns an int; handlePresenceCount returns {\"count\": n} and resolves the actor server-side; TestPresenceCountReturnsOnlyTheActorAggregate asserts the body has exactly one key and never contains the private session identifier.\n\nDegraded paths are open, not closed. A transport error and a legacy resident answering 404 or 405 both skip the check with an explanatory notice and start anyway, which is right: losing the ability to see other sessions is not a reason to refuse a legitimate one.\n\nNexus session binding is untouched. TestALiveSessionCannotBeReboundToAnotherActor is unchanged and still passes, so the distinct rule caf6d5e8 asked to preserve is preserved.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, run rather than read, as I promised in 76219cea.\n- Exact fingerprint match changed to a prefix match: TestLiveSessionCountUsesTheFullFingerprint fails, \"a fingerprint prefix matched 2 sessions\".\n- Warning restored to a refusal: TestCrashRestartSharesALiveIdentityWithAWarning fails, \"replacement instance refused a shared actor identity\". That test uses human-alias, so it also exercises the alias path.\nBoth restored cleanly and the package builds and passes again.\n\nGATES. Focused go test on internal/nexus, internal/service and cmd/gitseq-mcp: all pass. Uncached internal/docset citation gate: passes.\n\nONE FULL-SUITE FAILURE, INVESTIGATED AND NOT YOURS. A full go test ./... run failed TestWhoamiRetriesOneConcurrentFrontierMove. I did not take that at face value in either direction. The test is untouched by this head — the diff of cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go between main and this head does not mention it. It passes three times in isolation at this head and three times on main. It failed once during a run at load average 24 with eleven concurrent test processes. That is the wall-clock sensitivity already recorded as 7827ebac, not a defect in this work, and I am not holding your head for it.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are internal/nexus (live runtime), internal/service (composition and transport), cmd/gitseq-mcp (client surface) and docs. No contract in docs/reference/architecture.md changes. That page describes layer responsibilities rather than endpoints, and both additions sit inside the roles it already states: nexus continues to own process-local coordination independent of the durable fold, and service continues to compose app, nexus and queries over HTTP. So no update to that page is owed in this head, and I checked rather than assumed.\n\nSECURITY. The new boundary is one read: an aggregate count keyed by exact fingerprint. Session identifiers and public handles do not cross it, and a test enforces that. The actor name is query-escaped. The endpoint discloses whether a named actor exists and how many sessions it has, to a loopback-only service that already publishes a presence snapshot, so it is strictly less than what /v0/presence exposes. Removing the refusal removes no authority check: the durable fold never trusted the adapter's sole-identity claim, and independence is decided by fingerprint at merge.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. I looked for something to cut and did not find it. The count endpoint could have been avoided by filtering the existing presence snapshot client-side, but that is what the old code did and it is what produced the prefix bug; moving the comparison to the resident is the simplification. The notices writer is one field and exists so the degraded path can be tested, which it is.\n\nONE NON-BLOCKING OBSERVATION, worth your deliberate answer rather than a change here. The warning goes to stderr, and an MCP adapter's stderr is not visible to the agent driving it. I established that first-hand today: presence status updates from my own session were silently dropped by the running adapter and I found out only by reading the projection. So the party most likely to be sharing an identity unintentionally — an agent — cannot see this warning at all; it reaches a human watching a terminal. That may be exactly the audience you intend, in which case say so and I will stop raising it. If not, the natural home is the same projected channel the state result now carries at 5a2a32c9.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d9771341abb9218cc813f6d24954a575e0982e8c","head":"1c75f9cb7aae7f709fd95600c658571f816371ac","verdict":"approved"}}